From c4b29e01693cec1ff37a69baf9b3269100c1c17f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: JingMatrix <jingmatrix@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 16:47:22 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/47] Add Android 10 and 11 support via the legacy keystore
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Android 10 and 11 have no keystore2 or KeyMint: apps reach the keystore daemon over
its IKeystoreService binder, which sits above a Keymaster HAL. Rather than follow
that fragmented HIDL path, intercept the app-facing service directly. Injected into
keystore, we PLT-hook ioctl on libbinder, watch the incoming transactions, and
redirect the registered service binder to a local stub whose transactions an
in-process handler answers.

The handler serves a target app entirely from the reference TA. generateKey stores
the parameters; getKeyCharacteristics returns them; exportKey generates the key pair
and hands back its public key; attestKey imports that key into the TA with the app's
challenge, so the TA issues a keybox-signed attestation over the exact key the app
holds. Everything else is forwarded. The service replies through a callback binder
passed in each request, and the calls carry Keymaster parameters as a typed list, so
the handler mirrors those wire formats. It also supplies the tags the TA needs that
Android 10 leaves for keystore to add — creation and certificate validity dates, the
EC curve, and a synthesised attestation application id.

Because Android 10's libcrypto predates the BoringSSL the TA calls, the legacy
library bundles a modern static BoringSSL (CRYPTO_STATIC_LIB) instead of resolving it
from the host. The platform's C++ libbinder is not in the NDK, so it links stub
libbinder/libutils and vendors version-matched binder headers.

service.sh injects the keystore2 library on Android 12+ and this one on 10/11, and
resolves the target packages to uids (the unprivileged keystore cannot read the
package list). package.sh builds both interceptors for each ABI.
